                <pb facs="0903" n="869" xml:id="pg869"/>

                <head>372. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>President Johnson signed into law H.R. 653 (P.L. 90–638; 82 Stat. 1359), on
                    October 24, 1968. Section 2 established a uniform tariff on reprocessed wool
                    fabrics and blends based upon the chief weight of the principal component of the
                    fabric. The changes were scheduled to take effect 60 days after enactment, and
                    President Johnson directed the U.S. Tariff Commission to study alternatives. For
                    text of the President’s statement, see <hi rendition="#italic">Public Papers of
                        the Presidents of the United States: Lyndon B. Johnson, 1968–69</hi>, Book
                    II, pages 1071–1072.</p>
                <p>The tariff changes were not well received in Europe. European Economic Community
                    representatives said that textile trade arrangements negotiated in GATT were
                    imperiled. The Italian Government was especially displeased since its textile
                    trade with the United States was heavily impacted by the changes. (Telegram 9916
                    from Brussels, October 15, and telegram 9131 from Rome, October 26; both in
                    Department of State, Central Files, INCO–WOOL 17 US)</p>
            </div>
